"OWR-2021-5 (https://ems.press/content/serial-article-files/46883?nt=1)\nciteturn1288view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 76; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ems.press/content/serial-article-files/46883?nt=1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 3905\nL0@P0: Mathematisches Forschungsinstitut Oberwolfach\nL1@P0: Report No. 5/2021\nL2@P0: DOI: 10.4171/OWR/2021/5\nL3@P0: Logarithmic Vector Fields and Freeness of Divisors and\nL4@P0: Arrangements: New perspectives and applications\nL5@P0: (online meeting)\nL6@P0: Organized by\nL7@P0: Takuro Abe, Fukuoka\nL8@P0: Alexandru Dimca, Nice\nL9@P0: Eva-Maria Feichtner, Bremen\nL10@P0: Gerhard R¨ohrle, Bochum\nL11@P0: 24 January – 30 January 2021\nL12@P0: Abstract. The central topic of the workshop was the notion of logarithmic\nL13@P0: vector fields along a divisor in a smooth complex analytic or algebraic variety,\nL14@P0: i.e., the vector fields on the ambient variety tangent to the divisor. Following\nL15@P0: their introduction by K. Saito for the purpose of studying the universal un\u0002folding of an isolated singularity, this fundamental object has been the focus\nL16@P0: of studies in a wide range of mathematical fields such as algebra, algebraic\nL17@P0: geometry, singularity theory, root systems, (geometric) representation theory,\nL18@P0: combinatorics, (toric) topology, or symplectic geometry. In the last few years\nL19@P0: the logarithmic vector field approach has seen some unexpected and striking\nL20@P0: advances and deep applications. The aim of the workshop was to provide\nL21@P0: reports and to share these various new developments in the field.\nL22@P0: Mathematics Subject Classification (2010): 32S22; 52C35, 14N20, 13D02.\nL23@P0: Introduction by the Organizers\nL24@P0: The workshop Logarithmic Vector Fields and Freeness of Divisors and Arrange\u0002ments: New perspectives and applications, organised by Takuro Abe (Fukuoka),\nL25@P0: Alexandru Dimca (Nice), Eva-Maria Feichtner (Bremen), and Gerhard R¨ohrle\nL26@P0: (Bochum) was held as an online event due to the ongoing COVID-19 pandemic.\nL27@P0: The workshop was well attended with over 50 participants from around the globe.\nL28@P0: Overall, the workshop worked surprisingly well given the circumstances. The\nL29@P0: technical support through the video assistants, Lukas K¨uhne and Paul M¨ucksch,\nL30@P0-1: was excellent. All online talks were prepared extremely well. What helped greatly226 Oberwolfach Report 5/2021\nL31@P1: was the very good attendance and intense focus by participants. Naturally, the\nL32@P1: greatest challenge was posed by the vast difference in time zones of all the partic\u0002ipants locations, limiting participation on some morning and some late afternoon\nL33@P1: talks. But what seemed to work well is having a focused core session in the middle\nL34@P1: of the day allowing for most participants from outside Europe to participate.\nL35@P1: Also, what helped making this event a success was the fact that we were able\nL36@P1: to record all talks and that most participants were willing to share their slides.\nL37@P1: The topics of the contributions ranged from very classical topics such as sim\u0002plicial arrangements and singularities of configuration spaces to entirely new de\u0002velopments, such as elliptic reflection arrangements and Lagrangian geometry of\nL38@P1: matroids.\nL39@P1: We highlight some of the contributions in a number of areas separately showcasing\nL40@P1: the impact and relevance of the developments.\nL41@P1: Simplicial arrangements\nL42@P1: Michael Cuntz gave an overview on his recent joint work with S. Elia and J. Labb´e.\nL43@P1: Simplicial hyperplane arrangements generalize finite Coxeter arrangements and the\nL44@P1: weak order through the poset of regions. For simplicial arrangements, posets of\nL45@P1: regions are always lattices. The weak order is known to be congruence normal,\nL46@P1: and congruence normality for simplicial arrangements can be determined using\nL47@P1: polyhedral cones called shards. In their work they determine which arrangements\nL48@P1: lead to congruence normal lattices of regions. Using oriented matroids this work\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[1806.05295v1] A homological characterization for freeness of multi-arrangements (https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.05295v1)\nciteturn1288view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.05295v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 159\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Algebraic Geometry\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 13 Jun 2018]\nL9: # Title:A homological characterization for freeness of multi-arrangements\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Michael DiPasquale】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled A homological characterization for freeness of multi-arrangements, by Michael DiPasquale\nL14: \nL15: 【6†View PDF】 【7†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Building on work of Brandt and Terao in their study of $k$-formality, we introduce a co-chain complex associated to a multi-arrangement and prove that its cohomologies determine freeness of the associated module of multi-derivations. This provides a new homological method for determining freeness of arrangements and multi-arrangements. We work out many applications of this homological method.\nL17: For instance, we prove that if a multi-arrangement is free then the underlying arrangement is $k$-formal for all $k\\ge 2$. We also use this method to completely characterize freeness of certain families of multi-arrangements in moduli, showcasing how the geometry of multi-arrangements with the same intersection lattice may have considerable impact on freeness. New counter-examples to Orlik's conjecture also arise in connection to this latter analysis.\nL18: Comments:  | 37 pages, 7 figures\nL19: Subjects:  | Algebraic Geometry (math.AG); Commutative Algebra (math.AC)\nL20: MSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | Primary 14Q10, Secondary 13P20, 13D02, 13N15\nL21: Cite as:  | 【8†arXiv:1806.05295】 [math.AG]\nL22:    | (or 【9†arXiv:1806.05295v1】 [math.AG] for this version)\nL23:    | 【10†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1806.05295†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Michael DiPasquale [【11†view email】]\nL27: [v1] Wed, 13 Jun 2018 22:52:55 UTC (45 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled A homological characterization for freeness of multi-arrangements, by Michael DiPasquale\nL34: \nL35:   * 【6†View PDF】\nL36:   * 【7†HTML (experimental)】\nL37:   * 【12†TeX Source】\nL38: \nL39: 【13†view license】\nL40: \nL41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: math.AG\nL44: \nL45: 【14†< prev】   |   【15†next >】\nL46: \nL47: 【16†new】 | 【17†recent】 | 【18†2018-06】\nL48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: 【19†math】\nL52: 【20†math.AC】\nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * 【21†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL56:   * 【22†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL57:   * 【23†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (【24†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Connected Papers (【25†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com】)\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL83: \nL84: Litmaps (【26†What is L